e as no surprise to anyone who  knows me in real life but i didn't  expect my high key fear of basically  everything to show up in my genes as  strongly as it did i was also really  intrigued to find out that i have a lot  of genes that are associated with high  coffee consumption and a gene that is  associated with drinking less water  which might explain this what i liked  about self-decode is that along with  your health reports the company gives  you actionable insights on how to  improve your health based on what works  for your genes so for example apparently  people with my variant of the bdnf gene  see a lot of improvement in anxiety  symptoms with exercise so maybe i'll  start going for a run or hit the gym the  morning before i have to get on a  podcast self-decode's wellness reports  are full of useful recommendations for  diet and lifestyle changes and even on  potentially helpful supplements that can  help you optimize your personal health  that said self-decode is not a  diagnostic tool but it does give you  some genuinely useful insights that can  help you make wiser decisions when it  comes to taking care of yourself if  you're ready to learn more about your  genome and how to make the most of it  check out the link in the description at  checkout use the code wisecrack to get  25 off your self-decode order and now  back to the show now the human impulse  to find meaning in the stars is nothing  new ancient babylonians were reading the  night sky back in the second century bce  in ancient greece astrology developed in  tandem with astronomy and was treated  with respect by the likes of ptolemy and  plato throughout history kings would use  court astrologers to help them make  important decisions like who to invade  or what flavor ice cream to eat in the  12th century astrology became heavily  tied to medicine and the job title  physician astrologer was a real thing  astrology was even taught at  universities across europe until the  late 17th century but around then the  age of enlightenment coupled with  propaganda and political pressure put a  dent in astrology's credibility for a  couple hundred years it was relegated to  a fringe interest of freaky weirdos for  a long time in many places it was even  illegal to predict futures the  spiritualist movement of the mid to late  19th century would revive the ancient  craft somewhat but it would never be as  well respected fast forward to the  swinging 60s and baby boomers zealously  embraced astrology and to this day it's  remained a huge if hugely mocked part of  our culture but how did we get here what  accounts for astrology's enduring  popularity well you can largely thank  occult enthusiast alan leo a late 19th  century new ager who super liked reading  the stars but he had a big problem it  was still illegal in england to predict  the future for money so after going to  court and being fined for his little  hobby he took his lawyer's advice and  stopped making predictions but what good  was astrology without predictions of  death thunderstorms or unsightly pimples  on your wedding day leo was about to  figure it out see he also believed that  the age of aquarius was fast approaching  this was going to be a blissful era of  raised consciousness and humanity  reaching its true potential he wanted to  help everyone get ready for this dope  galactic spectacle specifically by  achieving better self-understanding  importantly leo believed that character  is destiny that men astrology should  help people better understand their  character and temperament rather than  predicting external happenings like car  crashes and here's a major plus  describing someone's personality is not  technically telling the future thus it  was not illegal now quick sidebar you  know your sun sign that's not your whole  astrological picture professional  astrologers take into account the  position of every single planet in the  solar system when crafting a star chart  but that is very complicated and  requires a lot of math and training thus  it was super expensive and pretty much  exclusive to the upper class this made  astrology kind of hard to popularize so  leo came up with the ultimate shortcut  teach people their sun sign and draw  conclusions about their character based  solely on that this bite-sized approach  to the craft proved massively popular  soon newspapers would start publishing  horoscopes and life would never be the  same for any of you two-faced geminis  just kidding but yeah he basically made  abbreviated horoscopes a thing though he  couldn't have possibly predicted the  21st century meme explosion he was  effectively setting in motion leo's sun  sign only approach would largely define  modern astrology standardizing zodiac  signs also helpfully made astrology  marketable on a massive level via cheap  almanacs conveniently this was happening  just as the consumer capitalist economy  was entering full swing at the same time  leo would inspire astrologer dane  rodyard to take this character stuff a  step further in the 1930s by invoking a  sparkly new type of science psychology  specifically by borrowing from one carl  jung side note turns out jung was a big  old weirdo who liked alchemy in  astrology in fact you liked astrology so  much that it's speculated that his zeal  for the craft was at least partially  responsible for his famous breakup with  mentor sigmund freud this makes sense  because as writers kieran lagrice and  saffron rosie explain in their book yung  on astrology at root both astrology and  union psychology might be seen as being  engaged with the critical task of  developing greater self-knowledge by  bringing to awareness the unconscious  factors underlying our life experience  rudyard formally merged astrology with  union psychology especially his notions  about the collective unconscious and  archetypes thus he created psychological  astrology and popularized the idea of  reading your horoscope as a cheaper  version of seeing a shrink rest assured  not everybody was happy about the  mainstreaming of astrology in 1952  cultural critic and notorious grump  theodor adorno wrote a semi-aggressive  78-page screed critiquing sunshine  columns he declared that fans of  astrology were persons who do not any  longer feel that they are the  self-determining subjects of their fate  he studied los angeles times horoscopes  and noted certain themes specifically  that they seemed to promote bourgeois  values like tact hard work and success  while flattering people about how  intuitive they supposedly were he  wondered how in the age of astrophysics  anyone could possibly believe that their  personality was affected by their date  of birth that same question continues to  be posed today for her part scholar  christine monk argues that two world  wars nuclear weapons and the  environmental crisis have all made  science seem a lot less sexy this then  leads people to seek out decidedly  unscientific things like astrology but  what if the popularity of astrology in  modern times isn't just explainable but  was actually pretty inevitable back in  1966 sociologist jacques matra argued  that astrology is actually a perfect fit  for modernity and many have since agreed  see as scholar marshall berman explained  rapid change is one of the central  tenets of modernity whether it's the  decreased power of religious and other  social institutions or rapidly  increasing globalization our world is in  a constant state of flux as monk  explains the world today seems more  enchanted than ever but is also in  certain ways more dangerous and  demanding we are constantly faced with  the challenge to make decisions and to  act in the world without full knowledge  and without a privileged point of view  frequent job changes cross-country moves  divorces and so on add far more  variables to everyday life than our  ancestors ever had meanwhile a decaying  social fabric leaves us all as lone  agents trying to figure out how to  behave this creates fraught situations  where we worry about making the wrong  choices about being the wrong kind of  person as scholar olaf hammer writes in  late modernity we construct who we  really are by engaging in self-reflexive  speculation and considering a variety of  options that's where astrology comes in  for a lot of people as a mediating force  to help them make sure they're not  [ __ ] up as monk puts it to use  astrology is a way to claim a home in  the cosmos that's during a time when a  lot of us don't have that psychic home  anywhere else that's why astrology is  particularly attractive to people in  liminal situations like a breakup for  job loss in a world full of choices the  guidance of the stars no matter how  unproven can feel centering for some  monk notes that astrology both lets us  feel acted upon by forces larger than  ourselves while also helping folks to  make a shift of perspective and to  reconstruct their worlds in slightly  different ways reading your horoscope on  the internet she explains can magically  create a foreground and background for  the self-reflective modern individual  who might feel ungrounded in their real  life in this way she believes its appeal  is largely about agency and empowerment  perhaps it's no wonder then that  astrology has in america traditionally  attracted marginalized groups like women  and people of color who are all too  often denied power in daily life some  scholars think astrology even functions  as a powerful coping mechanism this all  makes it somewhat ironic that astrology  has become an important spiritual outlet  for so many people precisely because  it's so aptly adjusted to modern  capitalism and mass production but the  popularity of astrology is also  reflective of the way society now sees  individuals remember how old school  astrology was all about big events like  wars and royal coronations rather than  about little old you and your  inexplicable romantic affinity for mouth  breathers that's in large part because  before the early modern era  individualism wasn't all that important  it was thought that every person had a  few basic core characteristics in common  but the concept of the unique individual  eventually caught on with a little help  from renaissance philosopher michel de  montagna among others in our late modern  times the pursuit of that authentic  unique self has become paramount and  astrology is one way of sussing out and  understanding your own quirks and  idiosyncrasies in the process hammer  explains we've seen decreasing concern  with cosmogonic myths or social charters  and an increasing insistence on  personalized legends self-centered  rituals and the spiritual transformation  of each individual the individualization  of religion of finding salvation in your  own self is distinctly modern and  perfectly suited to astrology and this  understandably coincides with the  overall reduction in power of organized  religions in our age hammer writes that  these days not only is truth to be found  within each of us the very locus of  spirituality is our own self but what if  your own self is kind of discombobulated  how can you make sense of your life well  astrology can help with that too  apparently as hammer explains astrology  facilitates something called narrativism  which conveys the idea that we tell the  stories of our lives stories that  construct a temporal unity for our  identities and that link our specific  life histories with purported grand  overarching themes in this way hammer  writes it offers a conceptual framework  for constructing a narrative unity of  life and furnishes a narrative form  within which one's life history can be  understood hammer even says these  narratives can function as therapeutic  interventions though please don't break  up with your therapist just because you  can blame all your problems on mercury  being a retrograde linguist charlotte  lin argues that narrative is one of the  most powerful ways of creating and  maintaining one's personal identity  narrativizing can also help you  establish causality and coherency in  your life story didn't get asked to prom  blame pesky venus didn't get that  promotion q mars essentially astrology  can help us explain away the apparent  randomness of human life and according  to lind it can do that regardless of if  we fully believe in it or not as monk  summarized in such a disorderly world  astrology offers a symbolic and  metaphorical system of imagining within  a frame of order even if that order is  you know not at all scientifically  proven and pretty randomly concocted by  a few savvy 20th century spiritual  entrepreneurs but what do you guys think  is astrology distinctly modern or a  lingering remnant of ancient  spirituality let us know what you think  in the comments big thanks to our  patrons for your support and be sure to  check out our podcast hit that subscribe  button with the fury of a vengeful  capricorn and don't forget to ring that  bell and as always thanks for watching  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]

